      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_6af5737cf81107af52037d9356eebec51f2eefc3b0647b6af5d52edcb32a5dd5",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The source chooses between two Voronoi representations according to the next proof operation. Oppenheim's full Bessel-series form retains the infinite representation but is only boundedly convergent on each fixed finite interval and is stated not to converge for a>3/4; Kiuchi's finite-cutoff form is introduced specifically as more convenient for an upper-bound estimate, with a choice of N producing Lemma 2. The truncation therefore gains an estimable finite representation while giving up the completeness of the full series. Because the displays are absent, the truncation error, the effect of N, the equality case a=3/4, and the quantitative proof of Lemma 2 remain unresolved.",
