    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3f22d19e86e063f405de3945bd4216201125f7889e4e04e57e0ab0d60ee1eee8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The full Voronoi series supplies the representation needed for the calculation but is boundedly convergent only on fixed finite intervals. The proof therefore cuts the improper integral first, uses bounded convergence to justify termwise integration on that finite range, applies a finite-cut saddle-point lemma, and removes the outer cut only after isolating the cutoff-dependent component and proving it vanishes while bounding the others. Here the finite integration cut is a legality device for the interchange, not merely a numerical approximation.",
